# Address-only K+2 hints on the winning rolling kernel

This private draft tests L1 and L2 hints separately while retaining the
rolling kernel's existing demand loads, carry copies and ordered MMA work.
It follows the v28 result: three register banks were byte-exact but lost all
twelve phase-1 comparisons across both measurement orders. Those timings are
in `outputs/window_threebank_v28.json` and
`outputs/window_threebank_v28_reverse.json`; they do not qualify this new
uncompiled experiment.

The new private entry point is:

```text
_inference_window_block_cache_hint(
    state, w1, w2, w3, qkv_weight, projection_weight,
    ffn_scale, attn_scale, head_scale, bias,
    phase=0, packed_output=True, raw_output=False, hint_level=1)
```

`hint_level=1` selects L1; `2` selects L2. Other values are rejected. The
existing C256 FP8 cache contract, half/byte input options, packed output,
optional raw-half output, graph/current-stream behavior, alignment handling
and inference-only restriction are preserved. All choice is private C++
template dispatch. There is no production policy or graph change.

At the top of each rolled iteration K, the new code issues an address-only
hint for K+2 when it exists, before the unchanged K+1 demand load. Each hint
uses exactly the same tile/lane address as that future demand load. For
C256, only K2 through K7 are hinted; K8 and K9 are never requested. The two
128-bit-vector addresses per lane are retained without a density or cache
layout change. This makes the L1/L2 comparison a single-variable experiment.

The assembly uses `prefetch.global.L1` or `prefetch.global.L2`, with no output
register operands, under `__CUDA_ARCH__ >= 890`. Its address input and memory
clobber do not create a read of the future B data. There are still sixteen
source B data words, compared with twenty-four in the rejected three-bank
trial. Extra address registers and instruction scheduling remain compiler
decisions. The existing architecture validation is copied from rolling.

The generator proves that removing the hint helper, its one guarded call and
template-argument plumbing restores the active rolling kernel exactly. This
includes W2, normalization, attention, publication, every actual B/A load,
carry, accumulator operation, shared write and all seven barriers. The CPU
address proof separately checks all expert/chunk, W3, Q/K/V and projection
subranges, and generic step counts 1–11. Hint events must precede the existing
next-B loads; deleting them must reproduce the original event stream.

For all C256 mappings, the proof counts 27,648 hint addresses and unchanged
36,864 vector B loads, 73,728 shared A reads and 294,912 lane MMA events.
The 442,368-byte sum of addressed vector spans is an address-coverage count,
not an estimate of cache-line traffic. Actual request merging, line fetching
and retention require compiled code and counters.

Files awaiting root activation/build:

- `tools/draft_window_cache_hint.py.draft`
- `csrc/kernel_impl/window_block_cache_hint.cuh.draft`
- `csrc/kernel_launcher/window_block_cache_hint.cu.draft`
- `csrc/torch_api/window_block_cache_hint.cpp.draft`
- `tools/window_cache_hint_proof.py.draft`
- `tests/test_window_cache_hint_schedule.py.draft`
- `tests/test_window_cache_hint.py.draft`
- `tests/benchmark_window_cache_hint.py.draft`

The benchmark's default three paths are rolling, L1 and L2. Each round
measures every path in a seeded shuffled order; `--reverse` reverses the same
round order. Weights/caches remain resident. Each graph holds distinct outputs
and is warmed before one CUDA-event sample per round. Graph construction and
cache preparation are excluded from resident timing. Returned bytes and
operand immutability are checked for every timing run; separate warmed graph
replays poison outputs before three correctness checks. `--all-candidates`
adds the other canonical, selected, cache, paired, ping-pong, three-bank and
QKV variants, for seventeen paths total.
The default result is a rolling-only screen: the all-incumbents performance
gate remains null unless all seventeen paths are measured.

After explicit activation and a successful build, the intended paired run is:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/benchmark_window_cache_hint.py --output outputs/window_cache_hint_v29.json
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/benchmark_window_cache_hint.py --reverse --output outputs/window_cache_hint_v29_reverse.json
```

The version label is illustrative until root chooses a build. GPU tests have
not run for this draft. They cover both hint levels, all four phases, batch
and spatial tails, half/packed inputs, optional raw outputs, real checkpoint
blocks 15/49, tiny/extreme publication patterns, offset/noncontiguous operands,
mutating CUDA graphs, nondefault streams, invalid contracts and autograd
rejection.

Before interpreting timing, inspect SASS for retained hint instructions,
actual K+2 address bounds, issue position relative to the later demand load,
unchanged MMA order, registers, spills and barriers. PTX `asm volatile` and a
memory clobber do not promise final SASS scheduling. A hint emitted beside
the demand load would fail the early-cache-warming hypothesis even if its
source position is correct. The earlier NCU evidence suggests testing L1
first, but neither hint guarantees data readiness or a speedup.

Only a useful measured hint should receive a fresh NCU study and native
physical-I/O comparison. It must beat the fastest measured incumbent by more
than 3% for its exact contract to be considered for later selection. The
full original-DLL speed requirement and 85% hardware-resource threshold
remain outstanding; local improvement alone does not pass them.
